as russia's war drags out in ukraine to  its west it's causing ripple effects in  central asia to its south the war has  made kazakhstan which is russia's  largest ally in the region increasingly  nervous and it has opened a door for  closer cooperation with the west here's  why  first let's take a closer look at the  map  kazakhstan borders the caspian sea but  since it doesn't have access to an ocean  it's considered the largest landlocked  country in the world in the neighborhood  are countries like russia  china turkey and iran over here is  western europe  that puts kazakhstan at the heart of  critical trade routes but it's also made  its economy heavily dependent on its  powerful neighbors the largest among  them is russia the former soviet sister  states shared this border which is  nearly 5 000 miles long that is the  second largest frontier in the world  after the u.s canada border and similar  to ukraine kazakhstan has a large ethnic  russian population about 22 percent and  many of them live along this northern  border  for years russian nationalists have  claimed that some of this land should  belong to russia similar to how they  perceive parts of ukraine that sentiment  has even been echoed by president  vladimir putin who said this about  kazakhstan's former leader in 2014  that sounds a lot like what putin has  been saying about ukraine and while that  kind of rhetoric was brushed off in the  past the invasion of ukraine has been a  wake-up call  to get a better sense of how the country  is responding i called my colleague evan  gershkovic who's been reporting from the  region they are worried extremely  worried about  what i think everybody has seen as an  increasingly erratic government in  moscow he says the country's oil  industry is a good example kazakhstan is  among the world's top oil producers and  take a look at its gdp growth you can  see that it tends to rise and fall along  with global oil prices the economy is  heavily reliant on oil sales  it has benefited from soaring oil prices  fueled by the war in ukraine and its oil  is seen as increasingly important to  europe which has begun to wean itself  off of russian fossil fuels but take a  look at this kazakhstan relies on this  caspian pipeline that runs through  russia to transport 80 percent of its  oil that reliance is a key vulnerability  according to researchers i talked to at  fitch ratings  russia has ordered the pipeline closed  twice in recent months the second time  came after kazakhstan's president kasim  jomar tokayev shared a stage with putin  and said that kazakhstan would not  recognize the breakaway republics of  luhansk and donetsk in ukraine  russia blamed alleged environmental  violations for the shutdown not those  comments but it rattled officials and  takayev ordered oil companies to explore  other ways to export the country's oil  it's clear that kazakhstan is freaked  out by this russia is still trying to  show who's in charge okay so oil is a  big deal but trade is another way that  kazakhstan has been impacted by the war  it has promised to enforce western  sanctions against russia a stance that  has anchored some in moscow but could  help kazakhstan become a critical trade  hub its ports on the caspian sea have  seen a surge in container shipments  compared to last year and the war has  drawn more attention to this so-called  middle corridor in the current war  situation it's the one main way to get  goods from china to europe that's not by  sea and so kazakhstan is trying to take  advantage of that the war in ukraine has  also changed how kazakhstan is thinking  about its security  in january when violent anti-government  protests broke out in kazakhstan tokyo  called in russian troops to help restore  order but now that russia has invaded a  former soviet state and neighbor  officials are rethinking their strategy  not only has the country boosted its  defense spending it has agreed to  strengthen military cooperation with  china according to china's ministry of  defense  and it also struck a deal with turkey a  nato member to jointly produce attack  drones kazakhstan has also had visitors  from the u.s including a top u.s  commander since the invasion u.s  diplomats have gone to central asia  multiple times so what we're seeing is  not necessarily this massive  geopolitical realignment but sort of  kazakhstan and the rest of central asia  hedging its  bets and the us seeing an opportunity  one central asia researcher told the  journal that the kremlin isn't troubled  just yet but if the government in  kazakhstan turns hostile it would be  seen as an even greater threat to russia  than ukraine since the countries share  such a long border kazakhstan's economy  isn't the only one reliant on russia  check out this video to learn more about  how europe could be impacted by a  natural gas war with moscow i'll see you  next time
 

